- How would you modify to also return the actual blocks boundaries when multiple optimal partitions exist?
- How does the solution change if limit is dynamic per block?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine weighted shortest paths with path counting via a DAG, a common real-world need in routing and logistics.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra for non-negative weights
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological DP to count paths
- Time/space trade-offs and modular arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for counting shortest paths
function countShortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const dist = dijkstra(graph, s);
  const dag = buildShortestPathDag(graph, dist);
  const order = topologicalSort(dag);
  const ways = new Array(graph.n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) {
    for (const [v] of dag.adj[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple target nodes efficiently?
- How does the solution change if edges can have zero weight, or if counts exceed 64-bit, and you need a different modulus or exact arithmetic?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests mastery of DAG shortest paths and cross-checks understanding of BFS/DFS-derived ordering vs Dijkstra's heap-based approach.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (DFS or Kahn)
- DAG single-source shortest paths via DP
- Edge relaxation and cycle detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function dagSSSP(n, edges, s){
  const g=[...Array(n)].map(()=>[]);
  const indeg=new Array(n).fill(0);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){g[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++;}
  const q=[]; for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  const topo=[]; while(q.length){ const u=q.shift(); topo.push(u); for(const [v] of g[u]){ if(--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);} }
  const INF=1e15; const dist=new Array(n).fill(INF); dist[s]=0;
  for(const u of topo){ if(dist[u]===INF) continue; for(const [v,w] of g[u]) dist[v]=Math.min(dist[v], dist[u]+w);} 
  return dist;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify for multiple sources? 
- How would you handle cycles or return -1 when unreachable?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses dynamic programming fundamentals through practical string segmentation, testing understanding of both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic Programming: memoization vs. tabulation
- String matching optimization (startsWith, trie)
- Time and space trade-offs between DP approaches
- State definition and transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function minWordSegmentsTopDown(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
  memo[n] = 0;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    let minSegs = Infinity;
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        const result = dfs(i + word.length);
        if (result !== -1) {
          minSegs = Math.min(minSegs, 1 + result);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[i] = minSegs === Infinity ? -1 : minSegs;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulated solution
function minWordSegmentsBottomUp(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  dp[n] = 0;

  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        if (dp[i + word.length] !== Infinity) {
          dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], 1 + dp[i + word.length]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[0];
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time:** O(n × m × L) where n = |s|, m = dict size, L = avg word length. Using a Trie reduces this to O(n × L).
- **Space:** O(n) for the memo/DP array.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a practical data structure for real-world features (auto-complete) and how to extend it with per-word frequencies.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequency tracking
- DFS/BFS to collect candidate words from a prefix
- Sorting by frequency then lexicographic order
- Edge cases: missing prefix, zero matches, tie handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.freq = 0; // frequency for word ending here
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.freq += 1;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (n, path) => {
      if (n.freq > 0) results.push([path, n.freq]);
      for (const [ch, child] of n.next) dfs(child, path + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, prefix);
    results.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return results.slice(0, K).map(v => v[0]);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prune rarely-used branches to save memory?
- How would you support concurrent inserts and autocomplete in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine topo sort with DP and counting paths; ensures handling of unreachable nodes and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting
- DP on DAG
- Shortest path counting modulo
- Edge relaxation and correctness sketch

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathsInDAG(n, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>[]);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u,v,w] of edges) { adj[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++; }
  const topo = [];
  const q = [];
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  for (let i=0;i<q.length;i++) {
    const u = q[i]; topo.push(u);
    for (const [v] of adj[u]) if (--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);
  }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const ways = Array(n).fill(0);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  dist[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u]===INF) continue;
    for (const [v,w] of adj[u]) {
      const nd = dist[u] + w;
      if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; }
      else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD; }
    }
  }
  return {dist, ways};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this if edges can have negative weights but the graph remains a DAG? 
- How to extend to output the K smallest distances with counts efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on strings, demonstrating memoization vs tabulation and optimization tricks.

## Key Concepts
- Pattern matching with Trie to prune dictionary checks
- Top-down vs bottom-up DP on string indices
- Handling empty inputs and modulo arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWays(s, dict){
  const trie = buildTrie(dict);
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[n] = 1;
  for(let i = n-1; i >= 0; --i){
    let node = trie;
    for(let j = i; j < n; j++){
      const c = s[j];
      if(!node.next[c]) break;
      node = node.next[c];
      if(node.end) dp[i] += dp[j+1];
    }
  }
  return dp[0];
}
function buildTrie(words){
  const root = {next:{}, end:false};
  for(const w of words){
    let node = root;
    for(const ch of w){
      if(!node.next[ch]) node.next[ch] = {next:{}, end:false};
      node = node.next[ch];
    }
    node.end = true;
  }
  return root;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to count parses modulo 1e9+7 for very large dictionaries?
- How would you adapt if wordDict contains duplicates or extremely long words?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to extend Dijkstra's algorithm to count shortest paths while handling ties and modular arithmetic—a common pattern in competitive programming and graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm with path counting augmentation
- Non-negative edge weights, including zero-weight edges
- Modular arithmetic for large path counts and overflow prevention
- Edge case handling: unreachable destinations, multiple optimal paths, zero-weight cycles

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, edges, s, t) {
  const MOD = 1e9 + 7;
  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);

  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;

  const pq = new MinPriorityQueue({ priority: x => dist[x] });
  pq.enqueue(s);

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const u = pq.dequeue().element;

    for (const [v, w] of edges[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        ways[v] = ways[u];
        pq.enqueue(v);
      } else if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], paths: ways[t] };
}
```

## Time & Space Complexity
- **Time**: O((N + M) log N) due to priority queue operations
- **Space**: O(N) for distance and path count arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Design a structure to maintain the k-th largest element efficiently in a streaming context, using a heap and a fixed-size container.

## Key Concepts
- Min-heap for thresholding top-k
- Time: O(log k) per insert
- Edge cases: fewer than k elements, duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class KthLargest {
  constructor(k, nums = []) {
    this.k = k; this.heap = [];
    for (const n of nums) this.add(n);
  }
  add(val) {
    if (this.heap.length < this.k) { this.heap.push(val); this._bubbleUp(this.heap.length-1); }
    else if (val > this.heap[0]) { this.heap[0] = val; this._siftDown(0); }
    return this.heap.length === this.k ? this.heap[0] : null;
  }
  // ... helpers omitted for brevity
}
```

