- What changes if k can grow or shrink over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced DP: partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums and both memoized and tabulated solutions. Demonstrates recurrence derivation, base cases, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Time: O(N^2 K); Space: O(NK) (or O(N))
- Edge-case handling for N < K

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, K){
  const N = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<N;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+A[i];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++) dp[i][1]=(pref[i]-pref[0])**2;
  for(let k=2;k<=K;k++){
    for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
      for(let j=k-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + (pref[i]-pref[j])**2);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][K];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you apply divide-and-conquer optimization if the cost function supports it?
- How would you adapt for at-most K segments or large N with memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to adapt sorting techniques to handle duplicate elements efficiently and to analyze complexity trade-offs between quicksort variants and mergesort.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partition using Dutch national flag algorithm
- Average vs worst-case complexity with duplicate keys
- In-place sorting with tail recursion optimization
- Space complexity comparison between quicksort and mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi) with lt/gt pointers
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  const pivot = a[lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;

  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) swap(a, lt++, i++);
    else if (a[i] > pivot) swap(a, i, gt--);
    else i++;
  }

  // Tail recurse on smaller subarray for O(log n) space
  if (gt - lo < hi - lt) {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt + 1;
  } else {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
    hi = lt - 1;
  }

  if (lo < hi) threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi);
}
```

## Test Plan
- **All equal**: Verify no swaps and linear time
- **Already sorted**: Confirm correct partitioning and maintain sorted order
- **No duplicates**: Validate against standard quicksort behavior
- **Random with duplicates**: Compare against built-in sort for correctness
- **Large duplicate ratio**: Measure performance improvement vs standard quicksort

## Complexity Comparison
- **Three-way quicksort**: O(n log m) average, O(n²) worst, O(log n) space
- **Standard mergesort**: O(n log n) always, O(n) extra space

Optimal when m ≪ n (many duplicate keys).

## Why This Is Asked
This tests building a Trie and adding an autocomplete-style suggest method, a common real-world need.

## Key Concepts
- Trie nodes with 26 children; end-of-word markers; storing full word for suggestions
- Lexicographic DFS for deterministic suggestions
- Complexity and memory trade-offs; edge cases like no matches

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor(){ this.children = new Map(); this.isWord=false; this.word=null }
}
class Trie {
  constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word){ let n=this.root; for (let ch of word){ if(!n.children.has(ch)) n.children.set(ch,new TrieNode()); n=n.children.get(ch); } n.isWord=true; n.word=word; }
  search(word){ let n=this.root; for(let ch of word){ if(!n.children.has(ch)) return false; n=n.children.get(ch); } return n.isWord; }
  startsWith(prefix){ let n=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!n.children.has(ch)) return false; n=n.children.get(ch); } return true; }
  suggest(prefix, limit){ let n=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ if(!n.children.has(ch)) return []; n=n.children.get(ch); }
    const res=[];
    const dfs=(cur, path)=>{ if(res.length>=limit) return; if(cur.isWord) res.push(prefix+path); const keys=Array.from(cur.children.keys()).sort(); for(let c of keys){ dfs(cur.children.get(c), path+c); } };
    dfs(n, '');
    return res;
  }
}
```

